originality preference 55-64% across judges\n5. Scaled replication n=350 — Likert reaches significance p=0.030, d=0.12\n6. Register axis GPT-4o — BT NS, GEE shows originality-dominated\n7. Rewriter robustness — Llama NLI drops 16pp, BT goes NS\n8. GPT-4o verbosity scoring — Likert significant p=0.005, breaking blindspot for this cell\n\n## Your task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Soundness of methodology** (1-5): Is the CSD pipeline well-designed? Are the statistical methods appropriate? Are there methodological flaws?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor** (1-5): Are experiments well-controlled? Are conclusions supported by evidence? Are there confounds not addressed?\n\n3. **Specific issues to flag**:\n   - The NLI verification gate: is bidirectional entailment sufficient for content preservation? What about subtle semantic shifts NLI might miss?\n   - GEE model specification: only two predictors (style, originality). Are there omitted variables? Is the model underspecified?\n   - Sample sizes: n=88-100 for most cells. Power concerns?\n   - Cross-benchmark failure: is it pipeline failure or genuine null? The paper acknowledges this but doesn't resolve it.\n   - Rewriter dependence: 16pp NLI drop with Llama. How does this affect pipeline validity?\n   - Originality confound: 55-64% across judges. Is GEE sufficient to control for this?\n   - Scaled replication: Likert reaches p=0.030 at n=350 with d=0.12 — does this undermine the \"efficiency gap\" framing?\n   - Evidence matrix: only 3/5 evaluable cells show PW-only pattern, 1 shows Both, 2 inconclusive. Is this enough to support claims?\n   - BT estimate regression (63% → 59% at n=350): winner's curse implications?\n\n4. **Missing experiments**: What experiments would strengthen the paper? Consider whether the paper covers the planned experiments adequately.\n\n5. **Reproducibility** (1-5): Can results be reproduced? Is enough detail provided?\n\n6. **Preliminary scores**:\n   - Soundness: X/5\n   - Excitement: X/5  \n   - Reproducibility: X/5\n   - Overall Assessment: X/5 (use the ARR scale above)\n\nFormat your review as a structured report with sections: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Missing Experiments, Scores. Be thorough and calibrated to EMNLP 2026 standards.","uuid":"680a647e-28cf-470a-a126-29c16af9fb74","session_id":"6668cb44-b8d1-40b2-9a3f-1ea035cc3371"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 review. Your focus: **methodology soundness and experimental rigor**.\n\n## Venue calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Main conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Accept rate ~21% for Main. Use half-point increments.\n\n## Paper summary\nTitle: \"Diagnosing Style Bias in LLM Judges: A Counterfactual Decomposition Approach\"\nThe paper proposes Counterfactual Style Decomposition (CSD), a five-stage pipeline: (1) axis-specific style rewriting, (2) NLI content preservation verification, (3) position-controlled pairwise comparison, (4) Bradley-Terry aggregation, (5) GEE logistic decomposition of style vs originality confounds. Applied across 4 judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Claude-Sonnet, Gemini-Flash), 3 style axes (formality, verbosity, register), 3 benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench).\n\nKey findings:\n- Style bias is model-specific: only 2/4 judges show significant formality bias\n- Likert sensitivity depends on axis observability: detects verbosity (surface-observable) but not formality even at n=350 (d=0.12)\n- GEE decomposition reveals confound gradient: verbosity=pure style (OR=13.10), formality=mixed, register=originality-dominated (OR=5.72)\n\n## Experiment design reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experiment plan includes 8 experiments:\n1. Multi-judge formality pairwise (4 judges on LLMBar, n=100)\n2. Cross-benchmark formality (AlpacaEval, MT-Bench, 3 judges) — all null after Holm correction, NLI pass drops to 72-78%\n3. Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity (systematic comparison) — GPT-4o verbosity Likert significant (p=0.008), breaking \"blindspot\" universality\n4. [not numbered in paper] — RATE control showing originality preference 55-64% across judges\n5. Scaled replication n=350 — Likert reaches significance p=0.030, d=0.12\n6. Register axis GPT-4o — BT NS, GEE shows originality-dominated\n7. Rewriter robustness — Llama NLI drops 16pp, BT goes NS\n8. GPT-4o verbosity scoring — Likert significant p=0.005, breaking blindspot for this cell\n\n## Your task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Soundness of methodology** (1-5): Is the CSD pipeline well-designed? Are the statistical methods appropriate? Are there methodological flaws?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor** (1-5): Are experiments well-controlled? Are conclusions supported by evidence? Are there confounds not addressed?\n\n3. **Specific issues to flag**:\n   - The NLI verification gate: is bidirectional entailment sufficient for content preservation? What about subtle semantic shifts NLI might miss?\n   - GEE model specification: only two predictors (style, originality). Are there omitted variables? Is the model underspecified?\n   - Sample sizes: n=88-100 for most cells. Power concerns?\n   - Cross-benchmark failure: is it pipeline failure or genuine null? The paper acknowledges this but doesn't resolve it.\n   - Rewriter dependence: 16pp NLI drop with Llama. How does this affect pipeline validity?\n   - Originality confound: 55-64% across judges. Is GEE sufficient to control for this?\n   - Scaled replication: Likert reaches p=0.030 at n=350 with d=0.12 — does this undermine the \"efficiency gap\" framing?\n   - Evidence matrix: only 3/5 evaluable cells show PW-only pattern, 1 shows Both, 2 inconclusive. Is this enough to support claims?\n   - BT estimate regression (63% → 59% at n=350): winner's curse implications?\n\n4. **Missing experiments**: What experiments would strengthen the paper? Consider whether the paper covers the planned experiments adequately.\n\n5. **Reproducibility** (1-5): Can results be reproduced? Is enough detail provided?\n\n6. **Preliminary scores**:\n   - Soundness: X/5\n   - Excitement: X/5  \n   - Reproducibility: X/5\n   - Overall Assessment: X/5 (use the ARR scale above)\n\nFormat your review as a structured report with sections: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Missing Experiments, Scores. Be thorough and calibrated to EMNLP 2026 standards."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HCHAwzsHRvvesX9nb28xmb","session_id":"6668cb44-b8d1-40b2-9a3f-1ea035cc3371","uuid":"42846196-3167-4cce-ac24-c4dc8e39166c","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:05:39.758Z"}
