{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UoRtpuy4kREss9NFUVjjjZ","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/memory.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Memory — causal_style_decomp_judge\\\\n\\\\n## 服务器环境 (westb-29275)\\\\n- SSH: `causal_style_decomp_judge`（~/.ssh/config 别名，免密）\\\\n- GPU: 4× RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell 96GB, torch=2.11.0+cu130\\\\n- 环境: aml-train 镜像, conda base (`source /root/miniconda3/etc/profile.d/conda.sh && conda activate base`)\\\\n- 数据盘: `/root/autodl-tmp/` 250GB\\\\n- **网络**: AutoDL 网络受限，每次 SSH 会话需 `source /etc/network_turbo`\\\\n- **CUDA JIT**: `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` 需包含 `/root/miniconda3/lib/python3.12/site-packages/nvidia/cu13/lib`\\\\n- **GPU 3 有驱动异常**（100% util/0 MiB），不可用，只用 GPU 0-2\\\\n- **HF 网络**: 必须设 `TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE=1 HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1` 避免 HF Hub 请求挂起\\\\n\\\\n## 模型路径\\\\n- NLI: `/root/autodl-tmp/models/nli-deberta-v3-large/`\\\\n- BERTScore: `microsoft/deberta-xlarge-mnli` 在 HF cache `/root/autodl-tmp/.hf_cache/`\\\\n- Qwen3-8B: `/root/autodl-tmp/models/Qwen3-8B/`\\\\n- Qwen3-32B: `/root/autodl-tmp/models/Qwen3-32B/` ✅ 完成（17 shards, 62GB）\\\\n\\\\n## API 配置\\\\n- **统一 proxy endpoint**: `http://47.94.22.126/v1`\\\\n- GPT-4o: key_id=`openai_api_key_direct` — ✅ 可用\\\\n- Claude Sonnet 4: key_id=`claude_api_key` — ❌ 代理不支持\\\\n- Gemini 2.0 Flash: key_id=`gemini_api_key` — ❌ 代理不支持\\\\n- **只有 OpenAI 可通过代理使用**，Claude/Gemini 需直接 API key\\\\n\\\\n## 数据\\\\n- MVP 数据: `/root/causal_style_decomp_judge/data/mvp_samples.jsonl` (100 条, seed=42)\\\\n- Pipeline 输出目录: `/root/causal_style_decomp_judge/data/`\\\\n- exp-005 RATE control: `data/rate_control_input.jsonl` (88 条, NLI≥0.90 过滤)\\\\n- Verified datasets: `verified_32b.jsonl`(100), `verified_verbosity.jsonl`(86 NLI-pass), `verified_register.jsonl`(94 NLI-pass), `verified_alpacaeval.jsonl`(86 NLI-pass), `verified_mtbench.jsonl`(73 NLI-pass)\\\\n\\\\n## RATE 方法 axis 可行性梯度（核心发现）\\\\n\\\\n| Axis | 改变了什么 | NLI 策略 | Pass rate | 结论 |\\\\n|------|-----------|----------|-----------|------|\\\\n| Formality | 表面 register | Bidirectional min(fwd,bwd) | 88% ✅ | 完全可行 |\\\\n| Verbosity | 信息量 | Direction-aware (D012/D013) | 86% ✅ | 需 NLI-only gate + direction-aware |\\\\n| Register | 话语情境 | NLI-only (D019) | 95% ✅ | NLI-only gate，与 formality 正交 (50%) |\\\\n| Tone | 认知承诺 | Bidirectional | 45% ❌ | 结构性不可行 |\\\\n\\\\n**D023 决策：所有 axis 统一使用 NLI-only gating**（threshold 0.90）。\\\\n\\\\n## NLI Gate 特性（exp-013 发现）\\\\n\\\\nNLI gate (DeBERTa-v3-large, threshold 0.90) 是 **precision-oriented conservative filter**：\\\\n- Precision 98.9%, Recall 91.6%, PABAK=0.82, AC1=0.89\\\\n- Kappa paradox（Feinstein & Cicchetti 1990）：base rate 88:12 → kappa=0.43 低估\\\\n\\\\n## 完整 2×3 矩阵（C1/C2/C3/C4 核心 evidence）\\\\n\\\\n### Blindspot 矩阵\\\\n\\\\n| Judge | Axis | BT P(style) | BT p | Likert p | Tie Rate | Blindspot? |\\\\n|-------|------|-------------|------|----------|----------|------------|\\\\n| GPT-4o | Formality | 63.0% | <0.001 | 0.332 | 62.5% | ✅ |\\\\n| GPT-4o | **Verbosity** | **76.0%** | **0.0001** | **0.403** | 58.1% | **✅** |\\\\n| GPT-4o | Register | 57.0% | 0.04 | 0.785 | 64.9% | ✅ |\\\\n| Qwen3-32B | Formality | 66.5% | <0.0001 | 0.908 | 72.7% | ✅ |\\\\n| Qwen3-32B | **Verbosity** | **77.3%** | **<0.0001** | **0.179** | 58.1% | **✅** |\\\\n| Qwen3-32B | Register | 59.3% | 0.220 | 0.505 | 63.8% | — |\\\\n\\\\n**5/6 cells Blindspot confirmed**。唯一例外：Qwen3-32B register pairwise p=0.22 不显著。\\\\n\\\\n### GEE Decomposition 完整 Gradient\\\\n\\\\n| Judge | Axis | Style OR | Style p | Orig OR | Orig p | 解读 |\\\\n|-------|------|----------|---------|---------|--------|------|\\\\n| Qwen3-32B | **Verbosity** | **13.10** | 2.6e-09 | 0.49 | 0.096 ns | **纯 style bias** |\\\\n| GPT-4o | **Verbosity** | **10.97** | <0.0001 | 0.44 | 0.051 ns | **纯 style bias** |\\\\n| Qwen3-32B | Formality | 4.20 | 0.0002 | 4.70 | <0.0001 | Style ≈ Orig |\\\\n| GPT-4o | Formality | 2.21 | 0.010 | 1.82 | 0.052 | Style > Orig |\\\\n| GPT-4o | Register | 1.88 | 0.028 | 2.21 | 0.006 | Orig > Style |\\\\n| Qwen3-32B | Register | 1.91 | 0.046 | 5.72 | <0.0001 | Orig >> Style |\\\\n\\\\n### 跨模型一致性（论文核心 argument）\\\\n- **Verbosity**: 两模型 Style OR=10-13，Orig OR<1 ns → **纯 style bias 是 model-independent**\\\\n- **Register**: 两模型 Style OR≈1.9 → **几乎相同的边缘 style effect**\\\\n- **Formality**: Qwen3-32B 双效应更强但 pattern 一致\\\\n\\\\n### C3 Axis Gradient\\\\n- **Verbosity = 纯 style bias**（OR=10-13，originality ns 且反向）\\\\n- **Formality = 混合效应**（style + originality 共存）\\\\n- **Register = originality 主导**（style 边缘）\\\\n\\\\n## Verbosity vs Formality 的方向不对称性差异（关键机制洞察）\\\\n\\\\n**Verbosity = 真正的 style bias**（非 originality bias）：\\\\n- verbose→concise: 80.6% prefer original（verbose）\\\\n- concise→verbose: 90.7% prefer rewrite（verbose）\\\\n- 两个方向都偏好 verbose content → 真正的 verbosity preference\\\\n\\\\n**Formality = 可能混合 originality bias**：\\\\n- formal→casual: 81.4% prefer original (p<0.0001)\\\\n- casual→formal: 50.0% (p=1.0, 无偏好)\\\\n- GPT-4o 模式类似但不极端：78.8% vs 54.5%\\\\n\\\\n**Register = 主要是 originality bias**：\\\\n- academic→conv: 89.3% prefer original\\\\n- conv→academic: 68.8% prefer original\\\\n\\\\n## BT Canonical 数字 (D032)\\\\n\\\\n- **NLI-filtered (primary)**: P(formal)=63.0%, CI [55.8%, 70.3%], p<0.001, 88 pairs/173 comp\\\\n- **All-data (robustness)**: P(formal)=60.9%, CI [53.8%, 67.8%], p=0.001, 100 pairs/197 comp\\\\n- Bootstrap: pair-level resampling\\\\n\\\\n## Power Curve Canonical (D030/D031)\\\\n\\\\n| Method | Effect Size | 80% Power n |\\\\n|--------|------------|-------------|\\\\n| Pairwise BT | p=0.630 | 114 |\\\\n| Pairwise Sign Test | p=0.739, r=0.523 | 62 |\\\\n| Binarized Likert | p=0.606, tie=0.625 | 462 |\\\\n| Likert ATE | d=0.073 (raw) | 1,473 |\\\\n\\\\n## 分析脚本路径\\\\n- BT + GEE (Qwen3-32B formality/register): `csd_pipeline/qwen32b_analysis.py`\\\\n- Verbosity GEE (Qwen3-32B): `csd_pipeline/verbosity_gee.py`\\\\n- GPT-4o 全量分析: `csd_pipeline/gpt4o_analysis.py`\\\\n- Power curve: `csd_pipeline/gen_power_curve.py`\\\\n- BT 主分析 (GPT-4o formality): `csd_pipeline/exp016_bradley_terry.py`\\\\n- Pipeline diagram: `csd_pipeline/gen_pipeline_figure.py`\\\\n- Forest plot: `csd_pipeline/gen_forest_plot.py`\\\\n\\\\n## 踩坑经验\\\\n- [2026-05-20] vLLM Qwen3 需要 `VLLM_USE_FLASHINFER_SAMPLER=0`（Blackwell sm120 不兼容）\\\\n- [2026-05-20] 32B FP16 单卡 96GB OK（gpu-memory-utilization=0.90 用了 88GB）\\\\n- [2026-05-20] Pairwise position-controlled dual-trial 比 Likert 灵敏得多\\\\n- [2026-05-20] Tone axis 结构性不可行\\\\n- [2026-05-21] NLI-only gating 对所有 axis 都适用（D023）\\\\n- [2026-05-21] Power curve 用 raw d=0.073（不依赖 RATE 假设）\\\\n- [2026-05-21] Qwen3-32B tp=2 可作为 local judge，~13 分钟 3 axis\\\\n- [2026-05-21] GEE decomposition 用 exchangeable correlation（statsmodels GEE）\\\\n- [2026-05-21] vLLM kill 必须用 specific PID，不能 killall（M006 教训）\\\\n- [2026-05-21] 代理 endpoint 只支持 OpenAI，不支持 Claude/Gemini\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 6100, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HCHAwzsHRvvesX9nb28xmb","session_id":"6668cb44-b8d1-40b2-9a3f-1ea035cc3371","uuid":"2a5cbd76-a740-4bb5-a9e5-d77c6a9ab5ae","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:05:58.048Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EqEZdBGQzVrMpZPgPbJ8Gf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Bsn2XSA4ECLQpjs2VuU8SC","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 review. Your focus: **novelty, positioning, and related work coverage**.\n\n## Venue calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Main conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Accept rate ~21% for Main. Use half-point increments.\n\n## Paper summary\nTitle: \"Diagnosing Style Bias in LLM Judges: A Counterfactual Decomposition Approach\"\nProposes Counterfactual Style Decomposition (CSD), a five-stage pipeline for diagnosing per-axis style bias in LLM judges. Stages: (1) axis-specific style rewriting, (2) NLI content preservation, (3) position-controlled pairwise comparison, (4) Bradley-Terry aggregation, (5) GEE logistic decomposition of style vs originality.\n\nThree claims:\nC1: Style bias is model-specific (2/4 judges show formality bias)\nC2: Likert sensitivity depends on axis observability (detects verbosity but not formality, even at n=350)\nC3: GEE decomposition reveals confound gradient (verbosity=pure style OR=13.10, formality=mixed, register=originality-dominated OR=5.72)\n\nRelated work section covers:\n- Style Bias: LC-AlpacaEval, SOS-Bench, Counterfactual Rhetorical\n- Measurement: Stevens 1946, DeCoster et al. 2011, RATE\n- Causal/counterfactual: Zmigrod et al. 2019, Vig et al. 2020\n\nKey experimental results:\n- 4 judges tested, only GPT-4o and Qwen3-32B show significant formality bias\n- Cross-benchmark (AlpacaEval, MT-Bench): all null after Holm correction, NLI pass rate drops to 72-78%\n- GPT-4o verbosity Likert is significant (p=0.005, d=0.31) — breaks \"blindspot\" pattern\n- Scaled replication n=350: Likert reaches p=0.030 with d=0.12\n- Register axis: originality-dominated in GEE, no BT significance\n- Rewriter robustness: Llama NLI drops 16pp, BT goes NS\n- Originality preference: 55-64% across judges\n\n## Your task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: \n   - How novel is the CSD pipeline? Each component (counterfactual generation, NLI verification, pairwise comparison, BT, GEE) is established. What is the contribution of combining them?\n   - Is the \"diagnostic tool\" framing sufficiently novel vs. existing work (LC-AlpacaEval, SOS-Bench, RATE, CASSE)?\n   - The GEE confound gradient: is decomposing style vs originality a genuine new insight or an expected result?\n   - The \"model-specific bias\" finding: is this surprising given existing literature?\n\n2. **Related work gaps**:\n   - Are important related works missing? Consider: FlatEval, JudgeBench, EvalBiasBench, Auto-Arena, preference bias literature (position bias, verbosity bias papers)\n   - Is the comparison to CASSE fair? They claim CASSE \"entangles causal factors\" — does CSD truly solve this?\n   - Is the DeCoster et al. 2011 citation appropriately used? It's about median splits, not Likert vs pairwise directly.\n   - Are there measurement theory works beyond Stevens 1946 that should be cited?\n\n3. **Positioning issues**:\n   - The paper pivots from \"blindspot discovery\" to \"diagnostic tool\" mid-paper. Is this narrative coherent?\n   - The claims are heavily hedged — model-specific, axis-dependent, benchmark-limited. Is there enough positive signal to constitute a contribution?\n   - Comparison to RATE: the paper suggests RATE's Likert choice \"may limit detection sensitivity\" but then shows Likert CAN detect verbosity. Is this positioning consistent?\n\n4. **Writing quality**:\n   - Is the paper well-structured?\n   - Are the 8 experiments clearly motivated and connected?\n   - Is the evidence matrix (Table 9) effectively communicated?\n   - Reference quality: are there any suspicious/hallucinated citations? Check citation formats.\n\n5. **Significance for the community**:\n   - Would EMNLP 2026 attendees learn something actionable?\n   - Is the practical guidance (Section 6.2) sufficiently grounded in the evidence?\n\n6. **Preliminary scores**:\n   - Soundness: X/5\n   - Excitement: X/5\n   - Reproducibility: X/5\n   - Overall Assessment: X/5\n\nFormat as: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Related Work Gaps, Scores. Be thorough and calibrated.","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":34061,"cache_read_input_tokens":23879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":34061},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6668cb44-b8d1-40b2-9a3f-1ea035cc3371","uuid":"3ac7d654-bc48-43bc-bb77-943b6f77e65e"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ab2578ddde73ece11","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bsn2XSA4ECLQpjs2VuU8SC","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 review. Your focus: **novelty, positioning, and related work coverage**.\n\n## Venue calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Main conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Accept rate ~21% for Main. Use half-point increments.\n\n## Paper summary\nTitle: \"Diagnosing Style Bias in LLM Judges: A Counterfactual Decomposition Approach\"\nProposes Counterfactual Style Decomposition (CSD), a five-stage pipeline for diagnosing per-axis style bias in LLM judges. Stages: (1) axis-specific style rewriting, (2) NLI content preservation, (3) position-controlled pairwise comparison, (4) Bradley-Terry aggregation, (5) GEE logistic decomposition of style vs originality.\n\nThree claims:\nC1: Style bias is model-specific (2/4 judges show formality bias)\nC2: Likert sensitivity depends on axis observability (detects verbosity but not formality, even at n=350)\nC3: GEE decomposition reveals confound gradient (verbosity=pure style OR=13.10, formality=mixed, register=originality-dominated OR=5.72)\n\nRelated work section covers:\n- Style Bias: LC-AlpacaEval, SOS-Bench, Counterfactual Rhetorical\n- Measurement: Stevens 1946, DeCoster et al. 2011, RATE\n- Causal/counterfactual: Zmigrod et al. 2019, Vig et al. 2020\n\nKey experimental results:\n- 4 judges tested, only GPT-4o and Qwen3-32B show significant formality bias\n- Cross-benchmark (AlpacaEval, MT-Bench): all null after Holm correction, NLI pass rate drops to 72-78%\n- GPT-4o verbosity Likert is significant (p=0.005, d=0.31) — breaks \"blindspot\" pattern\n- Scaled replication n=350: Likert reaches p=0.030 with d=0.12\n- Register axis: originality-dominated in GEE, no BT significance\n- Rewriter robustness: Llama NLI drops 16pp, BT goes NS\n- Originality preference: 55-64% across judges\n\n## Your task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: \n   - How novel is the CSD pipeline? Each component (counterfactual generation, NLI verification, pairwise comparison, BT, GEE) is established. What is the contribution of combining them?\n   - Is the \"diagnostic tool\" framing sufficiently novel vs. existing work (LC-AlpacaEval, SOS-Bench, RATE, CASSE)?\n   - The GEE confound gradient: is decomposing style vs originality a genuine new insight or an expected result?\n   - The \"model-specific bias\" finding: is this surprising given existing literature?\n\n2. **Related work gaps**:\n   - Are important related works missing? Consider: FlatEval, JudgeBench, EvalBiasBench, Auto-Arena, preference bias literature (position bias, verbosity bias papers)\n   - Is the comparison to CASSE fair? They claim CASSE \"entangles causal factors\" — does CSD truly solve this?\n   - Is the DeCoster et al. 2011 citation appropriately used? It's about median splits, not Likert vs pairwise directly.\n   - Are there measurement theory works beyond Stevens 1946 that should be cited?\n\n3. **Positioning issues**:\n   - The paper pivots from \"blindspot discovery\" to \"diagnostic tool\" mid-paper. Is this narrative coherent?\n   - The claims are heavily hedged — model-specific, axis-dependent, benchmark-limited. Is there enough positive signal to constitute a contribution?\n   - Comparison to RATE: the paper suggests RATE's Likert choice \"may limit detection sensitivity\" but then shows Likert CAN detect verbosity. Is this positioning consistent?\n\n4. **Writing quality**:\n   - Is the paper well-structured?\n   - Are the 8 experiments clearly motivated and connected?\n   - Is the evidence matrix (Table 9) effectively communicated?\n   - Reference quality: are there any suspicious/hallucinated citations? Check citation formats.\n\n5. **Significance for the community**:\n   - Would EMNLP 2026 attendees learn something actionable?\n   - Is the practical guidance (Section 6.2) sufficiently grounded in the evidence?\n\n6. **Preliminary scores**:\n   - Soundness: X/5\n   - Excitement: X/5\n   - Reproducibility: X/5\n   - Overall Assessment: X/5\n\nFormat as: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Related Work Gaps, Scores. Be thorough and calibrated.","uuid":"f55d1198-2cea-412c-b2c3-251b050c1b56","session_id":"6668cb44-b8d1-40b2-9a3f-1ea035cc3371"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 review. Your focus: **novelty, positioning, and related work coverage**.\n\n## Venue calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Main conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Accept rate ~21% for Main. Use half-point increments.\n\n## Paper summary\nTitle: \"Diagnosing Style Bias in LLM Judges: A Counterfactual Decomposition Approach\"\nProposes Counterfactual Style Decomposition (CSD), a five-stage pipeline for diagnosing per-axis style bias in LLM judges. Stages: (1) axis-specific style rewriting, (2) NLI content preservation, (3) position-controlled pairwise comparison, (4) Bradley-Terry aggregation, (5) GEE logistic decomposition of style vs originality.\n\nThree claims:\nC1: Style bias is model-specific (2/4 judges show formality bias)\nC2: Likert sensitivity depends on axis observability (detects verbosity but not formality, even at n=350)\nC3: GEE decomposition reveals confound gradient (verbosity=pure style OR=13.10, formality=mixed, register=originality-dominated OR=5.72)\n\nRelated work section covers:\n- Style Bias: LC-AlpacaEval, SOS-Bench, Counterfactual Rhetorical\n- Measurement: Stevens 1946, DeCoster et al. 2011, RATE\n- Causal/counterfactual: Zmigrod et al. 2019, Vig et al. 2020\n\nKey experimental results:\n- 4 judges tested, only GPT-4o and Qwen3-32B show significant formality bias\n- Cross-benchmark (AlpacaEval, MT-Bench): all null after Holm correction, NLI pass rate drops to 72-78%\n- GPT-4o verbosity Likert is significant (p=0.005, d=0.31) — breaks \"blindspot\" pattern\n- Scaled replication n=350: Likert reaches p=0.030 with d=0.12\n- Register axis: originality-dominated in GEE, no BT significance\n- Rewriter robustness: Llama NLI drops 16pp, BT goes NS\n- Originality preference: 55-64% across judges\n\n## Your task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: \n   - How novel is the CSD pipeline? Each component (counterfactual generation, NLI verification, pairwise comparison, BT, GEE) is established. What is the contribution of combining them?\n   - Is the \"diagnostic tool\" framing sufficiently novel vs. existing work (LC-AlpacaEval, SOS-Bench, RATE, CASSE)?\n   - The GEE confound gradient: is decomposing style vs originality a genuine new insight or an expected result?\n   - The \"model-specific bias\" finding: is this surprising given existing literature?\n\n2. **Related work gaps**:\n   - Are important related works missing? Consider: FlatEval, JudgeBench, EvalBiasBench, Auto-Arena, preference bias literature (position bias, verbosity bias papers)\n   - Is the comparison to CASSE fair? They claim CASSE \"entangles causal factors\" — does CSD truly solve this?\n   - Is the DeCoster et al. 2011 citation appropriately used? It's about median splits, not Likert vs pairwise directly.\n   - Are there measurement theory works beyond Stevens 1946 that should be cited?\n\n3. **Positioning issues**:\n   - The paper pivots from \"blindspot discovery\" to \"diagnostic tool\" mid-paper. Is this narrative coherent?\n   - The claims are heavily hedged — model-specific, axis-dependent, benchmark-limited. Is there enough positive signal to constitute a contribution?\n   - Comparison to RATE: the paper suggests RATE's Likert choice \"may limit detection sensitivity\" but then shows Likert CAN detect verbosity. Is this positioning consistent?\n\n4. **Writing quality**:\n   - Is the paper well-structured?\n   - Are the 8 experiments clearly motivated and connected?\n   - Is the evidence matrix (Table 9) effectively communicated?\n   - Reference quality: are there any suspicious/hallucinated citations? Check citation formats.\n\n5. **Significance for the community**:\n   - Would EMNLP 2026 attendees learn something actionable?\n   - Is the practical guidance (Section 6.2) sufficiently grounded in the evidence?\n\n6. **Preliminary scores**:\n   - Soundness: X/5\n   - Excitement: X/5\n   - Reproducibility: X/5\n   - Overall Assessment: X/5\n\nFormat as: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Related Work Gaps, Scores. Be thorough and calibrated."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bsn2XSA4ECLQpjs2VuU8SC","session_id":"6668cb44-b8d1-40b2-9a3f-1ea035cc3371","uuid":"38f84271-8294-42ce-b754-c73352bb29b6","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:06:02.832Z"}
