{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KgYdyqJ2KhyEULfQdB3mAY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Related Work}\n2\t\\label{sec:related_work}\n3\t\n4\tTwo lines of work motivate our approach: counterfactual methods for diagnosing judge bias, and measurement theory on the sensitivity of different elicitation formats.\n5\t\n6\t\\paragraph{Counterfactual Approaches to Judge Bias}\n7\t\\citet{reber2025rate} propose RATE, a framework that rewrites model outputs along style attributes using imperfect counterfactuals and measures the causal effect on reward model scores via Likert-scale Average Treatment Effect (ATE).\n8\tRATE applies a correction term to account for rewriting imperfections, but its reliance on Likert ATE as the primary metric is a vulnerability: our experiments show that Likert scoring exhibits a ceiling effect in which 60\\% of style-transformed pairs receive identical integer scores, yielding non-significant ATE estimates ($p = 0.332$) for the same interventions that pairwise comparison detects at $p = 0.006$.\n9\tIf this Likert Blindspot generalizes beyond our setting, RATE's own style-related findings may underestimate the true magnitude of attribute effects.\n10\tCSD differs from RATE in three respects: it targets individual style axes rather than general attributes, it measures bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison rather than Likert ATE, and it enforces content preservation via NLI entailment gating rather than post-hoc correction for imperfect rewrites.\n11\t\\citet{counterfactual2025rhetorical} also adopt a counterfactual LLM-based design, rewriting texts to measure rhetorical style effects and aggregating preferences with a Bradley-Terry model.\n12\tTheir framework operates at persona-level granularity (e.g., ``writes like a professor''), whereas CSD intervenes on specific style axes (e.g., formality, conciseness) and uses a sign test with position control rather than parametric aggregation.\n13\t\n14\t\\paragraph{Measurement Sensitivity in NLG Evaluation}\n15\tThe distinction between ordinal and interval measurement dates to \\citet{stevens1946theory}, whose taxonomy of scales remains foundational.\n16\tThe superiority of pairwise comparison over rating scales for eliciting reliable human judgments has been established since \\citet{thurstone1927law}, whose Law of Comparative Judgment formalizes why forced-choice comparisons yield finer discriminability than absolute ratings.\n17\tMore broadly, \\citet{decoster2011median} show that artificial categorization of continuous variables---including the binarization step inherent in converting Likert scores to preference decisions---discards variance and reduces statistical power, a finding directly relevant to the Likert Blindspot mechanism we identify.\n18\tWithin NLP, \\citet{kiritchenko2017bws} demonstrate that best-worst scaling produces more reliable sentiment intensity annotations than Likert scales, and \\citet{novikova2018rankme} show that ranking-based evaluation detects quality differences in natural language generation systems that rating-based methods miss entirely.\n19\tOur contribution is not the discovery of this principle but the first systematic quantification of its magnitude for LLM judge style bias detection, along with the binarized Likert diagnostic that isolates the mechanism (measurement granularity, not test selection).\n20\t\n21\t\\paragraph{Style Bias Detection and Mitigation}\n22\tThe foundational LLM-as-judge framework was established by \\citet{zheng2023judging}, who introduced MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena and documented position bias in pairwise evaluation.\n23\t\\citet{wang2023faireval} further showed that preference rankings can be manipulated by swapping option order, proposing a win/tie/lose annotation scheme.\n24\t\\citet{li2024calibraeval} address selection bias through calibration of prediction distributions during inference.\n25\tThese works focus on \\emph{position} bias and calibration; style bias as a separate, dominant bias source has received attention only recently \\citep{murugadoss2025sos,li2026preference,soumik2026judging}.\n26\tMulti-feature frameworks such as CASSE \\citep{casse2026concise} and FairJudge \\citep{wang2026fairjudge} decompose style into finer dimensions but rely on correlational analyses without interventional controls.\n27\t\n28\t\\paragraph{Bradley-Terry Models in Evaluation}\n29\tThe Bradley-Terry model \\citep{bradley1952rank} underpins modern LLM evaluation platforms including Chatbot Arena \\citep{zheng2023judging} and AlpacaFarm \\citep{dubois2023alpacafarm}.\n30\tWe adopt BT as our primary pairwise analysis because it uses all comparison data---including position-inconsistent pairs---without requiring a consistency filter, yielding proper effect-size confidence intervals that sign tests cannot provide.\n31\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HCHAwzsHRvvesX9nb28xmb","session_id":"6668cb44-b8d1-40b2-9a3f-1ea035cc3371","uuid":"048fcfb4-6840-4f31-a1e0-0dc36724921a","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:06:23.087Z"}
