{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GZzPdDYA3s8sVKaYWL5A4m","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,twocolumn]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n7\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n8\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n11\t\\usepackage{caption}\n12\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n13\t\\usepackage{times}\n14\t\n15\t\\setlength{\\columnsep}{0.6cm}\n16\t\\captionsetup{font=small,labelfont=bf}\n17\t\n18\t\\newcommand{\\bt}{\\textsc{bt}}\n19\t\\newcommand{\\cmark}{\\checkmark}\n20\t\n21\t\\title{The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison}\n22\t\n23\t\\author{Anonymous}\n24\t\\date{}\n25\t\n26\t\\begin{document}\n27\t\\maketitle\n28\t\n29\t% ============================================================\n30\t% ABSTRACT\n31\t% ============================================================\n32\t\\begin{abstract}\n33\tLarge Language Model (LLM) judges are widely used for evaluating text quality, yet their susceptibility to surface-level style features remains poorly characterized.\n34\tWe introduce the \\textbf{Counterfactual Style Decomposition (CSD)} pipeline, which generates style-only counterfactual pairs via constrained rewriting, verifies content preservation through Natural Language Inference (NLI), and measures per-axis style bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n35\tApplying CSD to four LLM judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, Gemini-2.0-Flash) across three style axes (formality, verbosity, register) and three benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), we identify a systematic \\textbf{Likert Blindspot}: pairwise comparison detects significant style bias in 16 of 18 judge$\\times$axis$\\times$benchmark cells (Bradley-Terry win probabilities 0.59--0.86, all $p<0.05$), while standard Likert scoring remains non-significant in all but 2 cells.\n36\tGeneralized Estimating Equation (GEE) decomposition reveals a \\textbf{confound gradient} across axes: verbosity bias is confound-free (style OR$=$10.5--13.1, originality NS), formality is mixed (both ORs 2--5), and register is originality-dominated (originality OR$=$4.2--5.7).\n37\tThis gradient replicates across judges and constitutes actionable guidance for practitioners: axis selection and confound decomposition are necessary steps when auditing LLM judge bias.\n38\t\\end{abstract}\n39\t\n40\t% ============================================================\n41\t% 1. INTRODUCTION\n42\t% ============================================================\n43\t\\section{Introduction}\n44\t\n45\tLLM-as-judge evaluation has become central infrastructure for both NLP benchmarking and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) alignment training.\n46\tRecent work demonstrates that surface-level style features, particularly response length, can dominate judge preferences: SOS-Bench reports a correlation of $R=0.999$ between style scores and overall judge ratings \\citep{sosbench2025}.\n47\tIf judges systematically prefer certain stylistic presentations independent of content quality, downstream model selection and alignment training inherit these biases.\n48\t\n49\tStandard practice evaluates LLM judge reliability using Likert-scale scoring, where judges assign absolute quality ratings.\n50\tHowever, the sensitivity of Likert scoring to style confounds has not been systematically examined."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bsn2XSA4ECLQpjs2VuU8SC","session_id":"6668cb44-b8d1-40b2-9a3f-1ea035cc3371","uuid":"ef3b5c81-00aa-4688-947a-178573c33c64","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:07:18.061Z"}
