n316\t        note: \"R1 提出；R2 修正\"\n317\t    action_items:\n318\t      - round: 1\n319\t        action: \"GEE for all 6 cells. Verbosity confound-free. Cross-axis gradient\"\n320\t        assigned_to: paper_agent\n321\t        status: done\n322\t    resolution: \"Per-cell GEE with cross-judge validation\"\n323\t    resolution_round: 1\n324\t\n325\t  - id: \"CROSS-3\"\n326\t    summary: \"Likert ATE 方向反转——测量不同构念\"\n327\t    category: experiment\n328\t    severity: warning\n329\t    status: resolved\n330\t    first_raised_round: 1\n331\t    last_seen_round: 1\n332\t    mentions:\n333\t      - round: 1\n334\t        reviewers: [1, 2]\n335\t        note: \"R1 发现；R2 升级\"\n336\t    action_items:\n337\t      - round: 1\n338\t        action: \"Reframed as magnitude gap + construct difference\"\n339\t        assigned_to: paper_agent\n340\t        status: done\n341\t    resolution: \"Magnitude gap framing, not directional claim\"\n342\t    resolution_round: 1\n343\t\n344\t  - id: \"CROSS-4\"\n345\t    summary: \"Ordinal regression 必要实验\"\n346\t    category: method\n347\t    severity: critical\n348\t    status: resolved\n349\t    first_raised_round: 1\n350\t    last_seen_round: 1\n351\t    mentions:\n352\t      - round: 1\n353\t        reviewers: [1, 2]\n354\t        note: \"收敛轮共识\"\n355\t    action_items:\n356\t      - round: 1\n357\t        action: \"exp-015 p=0.769 + 7-method hierarchy. Reviewer verified\"\n358\t        assigned_to: paper_agent\n359\t        status: done\n360\t    resolution: \"Definitively resolved by exp-015 + Table 2\"\n361\t    resolution_round: 1\n362\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bsn2XSA4ECLQpjs2VuU8SC","session_id":"6668cb44-b8d1-40b2-9a3f-1ea035cc3371","uuid":"e32c8d63-8a20-41db-a66f-61ac538d048f","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:07:43.787Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a9d202cd973b194ce","tool_use_id":"toolu_01HCHAwzsHRvvesX9nb28xmb","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":75872,"tool_uses":23,"duration_ms":175694},"uuid":"761a0903-82b1-43d9-8774-822469f1c46e","session_id":"6668cb44-b8d1-40b2-9a3f-1ea035cc3371"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HCHAwzsHRvvesX9nb28xmb","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my thorough reading of the paper, project files, experimental decisions, and evidence matrix, here is my structured review.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Methodology Soundness and Experimental Rigor\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper proposes Counterfactual Style Decomposition (CSD), a pipeline for detecting per-axis style bias in LLM judges through style-controlled rewriting, NLI content gating, and position-controlled pairwise comparison. The central finding is the \"Likert Blindspot\": standard Likert scoring systematically fails to detect style biases that pairwise comparison reveals clearly. Evidence spans 2 judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B) x 3 axes (formality, verbosity, register), with GEE logistic decomposition revealing a confound gradient across axes. The paper makes a measurement-methodology contribution rather than a novel algorithmic one.\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1. Well-controlled experimental design with systematic robustness checks.**\nThe seven-method sensitivity hierarchy (Table 2) is the paper's strongest methodological contribution. By showing that ALL Likert-based methods fail (ATE, binarized, ordinal regression, GEE, Wilcoxon) while both pairwise methods succeed, the authors convincingly rule out test-choice confounds. The ordinal regression result (p=0.769) is particularly important---it eliminates the interval-scale assumption objection definitively.\n\n**S2. Cross-judge and cross-axis replication with consistent patterns.**\nThe 2x3 matrix with both proprietary (GPT-4o) and open-source (Qwen3-32B) judges, showing consistent Blindspot patterns, is methodologically sound. The verbosity axis (BT 76-77%, both judges) provides a near-ideal demonstration because the GEE originality component is non-significant (OR=0.44-0.49), meaning the entire observed effect is attributable to style.\n\n**S3. Appropriate statistical framework.**\nThe use of Bradley-Terry as primary analysis with pair-level bootstrap is correct for position-controlled pairwise data. The Holm-Bonferroni correction with explicit primary/exploratory/confirmatory designation shows statistical maturity. The paper properly acknowledges the Hoenig & Heisey (2001) critique and reframes the power curve as illustrative rather than post-hoc power analysis.\n\n**S4. Honest treatment of limitations.**\nThe paper forthrightly discusses evaluator-producer bias (Qwen3-32B as both rewriter and judge), the register axis weakness, and the quality-style confound identifiability limit. The GEE gradient (verbosity=pure style, formality=mixed, register=originality-dominated) is presented as a diagnostic finding rather than overclaimed.\n\n**S5. Direction-aware NLI verification is a genuine methodological insight.**\nRecognizing that bidirectional entailment is structurally incompatible with information-density-changing axes (recovering verbosity from 65% to 86% pass rate) demonstrates careful thinking about the measurement pipeline's validity conditions.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1. (Major) GEE model specification is underspecified with only two predictors.**\nThe GEE logistic model includes only `style` and `originality` as predictors. This is a severely underspecified model for the phenomena being studied. Omitted variables include: text length (even after style rewriting), text complexity/readability, topic/domain effects, and judge-specific position bias residuals (position is controlled by design but may interact with style). The massive ORs for verbosity (10.97-13.10) suggest possible confounding with length---verbose rewrites are necessarily longer, and length bias is the most documented LLM judge bias. The paper does not separate the verbosity style effect from the pure length effect, which is a critical omission given that LC-AlpacaEval specifically targets length as the dominant confound. Without a length covariate in the GEE model, the claim that verbosity represents \"pure style bias\" is not supported---it may equally be pure length bias relabeled.\n\n**W2. (Major) Sample sizes raise precision concerns despite adequate power for primary effects.**\nn=88-100 per cell is sufficient for the observed large effects (BT 63-77%), but the GEE decomposition requires adequate power to detect the *absence* of the originality confound. Claiming verbosity is \"confound-free\" because originality OR=0.49 with p=0.096 is a failure-to-reject-null claim. With n=86 (verbosity NLI-pass), the power to detect a meaningful originality effect (say OR=2.0) is modest. The CI for Qwen3-32B verbosity originality OR ([0.21, 1.13]) includes values up to 1.13---it cannot rule out a small originality confound. The paper should present this as \"no evidence of confounding\" rather than \"confound-free.\"\n\n**W3. (Major) NLI gate as content preservation verification has fundamental limitations the paper underacknowledges.**\nBidirectional NLI entailment tests whether propositions in A are entailed by B and vice versa. This is necessary but not sufficient for content preservation: (a) NLI operates at the propositional level and may miss pragmatic/implicature shifts; (b) quality perception can shift without propositional content changing (e.g., a poorly-structured formal sentence may be perceived as lower quality than a well-structured casual one); (c) the paper acknowledges this as \"quality perception shifts mediated by style itself\" in limitations, but this is not merely a limitation---it is a fundamental identifiability problem that affects the core claim. If the judge perceives formal text as higher quality *because of* formality (style mediates quality perception), then OR=2.21 for formality conflates style bias with legitimate quality assessment. The NLI gate cannot distinguish these.\n\n**W4. (Moderate) Cross-benchmark failure is inadequately addressed.**\nThe paper mentions only LLMBar as the evaluation benchmark, despite the experimental plan including AlpacaEval and MT-Bench. The project files reveal that NLI pass rates drop to 72-78% on these benchmarks, and results become non-significant after Holm correction. This is buried in the paper as a limitation (\"additional axes, larger benchmarks... needed\") but should be transparently reported as a negative result. Does CSD's Blindspot finding generalize beyond LLMBar? The paper cannot answer this, which limits the practical significance of the contribution.\n\n**W5. (Moderate) Rewriter dependence undermines generalizability claims.**\nThe project files mention that a Llama rewriter shows a 16pp NLI pass rate drop (from 88% to an implied ~72%). If the pipeline's validity depends critically on using Qwen3-32B as the rewriter, this constrains the practical applicability. The paper does not report any rewriter robustness experiment, despite it being in the experimental plan. This is an important omission---if different rewriters produce different counterfactual quality, the detected \"bias\" magnitude may be partially an artifact of rewriter-judge interaction.\n\n**W6. (Moderate) The \"Blindspot\" framing overstates what is essentially a well-known statistical power difference.**\nThe core finding---that forced-choice pairwise comparison has higher discriminability than rating scales---has been established since Thurstone (1927) and is well-documented in psychometrics. The paper acknowledges this in Related Work (\"Our contribution is not the discovery of this principle but the first systematic quantification\"), but the title, abstract, and framing continue to present this as a \"Blindspot\" discovery. At n=350 (from the experimental plan), Likert reaches significance (p=0.030, d=0.12). This confirms the finding is a power/sensitivity issue, not a structural blindspot. The paper's own power curve shows that at n~462, binarized Likert would detect the effect. Calling this a \"Blindspot\" rather than a \"sensitivity gap\" is a framing choice that borders on overclaiming.\n\n**W7. (Minor) BT estimate regression (63% from n=88 vs. planned ~60% at larger n) suggests winner's curse.**\nThe project's decision log (D032) shows the BT estimate evolved from 69.1% to 63.0% as methodology was corrected. The dream review imagines further regression to ~64.8% at n=400. This trajectory is consistent with winner's curse / regression to the mean at small samples. While 63% remains significant, the CI width (14.5pp) is large enough that the true effect could be as low as 55.8%---barely above chance in practical terms. The paper does not discuss this.\n\n**W8. (Minor) Evaluator-producer bias is inadequately controlled.**\nQwen3-32B serves as: (1) style classifier, (2) rewriter, (3) NLI validator (via LLM-as-annotator), and (4) one of two judges. This quadruple role creates layered confounds. The paper argues GPT-4o provides independent validation, but GPT-4o is only one of two judges, and the rewriting artifacts are common to both judges' inputs. A rewriter from a different model family would have been essential for the primary results.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\nQ1. Can you add a length covariate to the GEE model for the verbosity axis? If the verbosity style OR drops substantially after controlling for token count, the \"pure style bias\" claim needs revision.\n\nQ2. What happens to the Blindspot if you use a continuous Likert scale (e.g., 0-100 slider) instead of integer 1-10? Your mechanism posits that integer discretization produces ties---have you considered that API judges could be prompted for finer-grained scores?\n\nQ3. The direction asymmetry for formality (78.8% vs 54.5%) and register (89.3% vs 68.8%) suggests that what you're measuring may be partially \"judges prefer original text\" rather than \"judges prefer formal/academic text.\" Your GEE controls for this, but with only 2 predictors and moderate n, how confident are you that the decomposition is correctly specified?\n\nQ4. Why not report AlpacaEval/MT-Bench results, even as negatives? The cross-benchmark generalizability is critical for the practical implications you draw.\n\nQ5. The paper claims the Blindspot is \"inherent to Likert measurement\" (abstract). But at n=462 your own power curve predicts detection. Is this not an \"inherent\" property of small-sample Likert rather than Likert measurement per se?\n\n### Missing Experiments\n\n1. **Length-controlled GEE for verbosity**: Add token count as a third predictor in the verbosity GEE. This is essential to distinguish \"verbosity style preference\" from \"length bias\"---the most documented confound in LLM judges.\n\n2. **Non-Qwen rewriter with full pipeline**: The experimental plan includes this (exp-009) but it does not appear in the paper. Even a pilot with GPT-4o as rewriter on 50 pairs would address the evaluator-producer concern.\n\n3. **Cross-benchmark results**: Report AlpacaEval and MT-Bench results transparently, even if null after correction. The absence of these results leaves a major gap in generalizability.\n\n4. **Continuous/fine-grained Likert**: Prompt judges for scores on a 0-100 scale or request logprob-based continuous scores. If the Blindspot disappears with finer granularity, the mechanism claim is validated; if it persists, the finding is stronger.\n\n5. **Human validation of NLI gate on a random sample**: The current validation uses Qwen3-32B (same model family as rewriter). Even 30 human-annotated pairs would substantially strengthen the NLI gate validity claim.\n\n6. **Additional judges (Claude, Gemini)**: The plan includes 4 judges but only 2 are tested. This limits the \"generalizes across architecturally distinct judges\" claim to a single comparison.\n\n### Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | 3.5/5 | Core methodology is reasonable but GEE underspecification (W1), NLI identifiability limits (W3), and the verbosity-length confound are significant gaps. Statistical methods are generally appropriate. |\n| **Excitement** | 2.5/5 | The contribution is a systematic quantification of a known psychometric phenomenon (pairwise > rating scales) applied to LLM judges. Useful but limited novelty. The GEE gradient is more interesting but underdeveloped and only has 2 judges x 3 axes. |\n| **Reproducibility** | 3.5/5 | Good detail on statistical methods, NLI thresholds, bootstrap parameters. Pipeline stages are clearly described. However: Qwen3-32B rewriting prompts are not fully specified, API model versions are given only for GPT-4o, and the code/data are not released. |\n| **Overall Assessment** | 3.0/5 | Findings-level contribution. The Likert Blindspot finding is real and practically useful, but (a) limited novelty given psychometric literature, (b) scope restricted to 1 benchmark x 2 judges, (c) the verbosity-length confound unaddressed, and (d) several planned experiments missing from the paper. With the missing experiments and a more honest framing (sensitivity gap rather than \"Blindspot\"), this would be a solid Findings paper. For Main, would need either broader scope (4+ judges, multiple benchmarks) or a deeper methodological contribution (the GEE gradient could serve this role if developed with proper model specification). |\n\n**Overall Assessment: 3.0 (Findings-level)**\n\nThe paper identifies a real and practically relevant phenomenon (Likert insensitivity to style bias in LLM judges) and provides solid within-scope evidence. However, the novelty ceiling (domain transfer of established psychometric principles), limited scope (1 benchmark, 2 judges), and the unaddressed verbosity-length confound prevent a Main conference recommendation. The GEE decomposition gradient is the most novel aspect but needs stronger model specification and broader validation to serve as a standalone contribution."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a9d202cd973b194ce (use SendMessage with to: 'a9d202cd973b194ce' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 78286\ntool_uses: 23\nduration_ms: 175698</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6668cb44-b8d1-40b2-9a3f-1ea035cc3371","uuid":"31a87092-665e-4865-8bc7-1c26df5597be","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:08:35.459Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 review. Your focus: **methodology soundness and experimental rigor**.\n\n## Venue calibration (EMNLP 2026 via ARR)\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Main conference accept\n- 3 = Findings accept\n- 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Accept rate ~21% for Main. Use half-point increments.\n\n## Paper summary\nTitle: \"Diagnosing Style Bias in LLM Judges: A Counterfactual Decomposition Approach\"\nThe paper proposes Counterfactual Style Decomposition (CSD), a five-stage pipeline: (1) axis-specific style rewriting, (2) NLI content preservation verification, (3) position-controlled pairwise comparison, (4) Bradley-Terry aggregation, (5) GEE logistic decomposition of style vs originality confounds. Applied across 4 judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Claude-Sonnet, Gemini-Flash), 3 style axes (formality, verbosity, register), 3 benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench).\n\nKey findings:\n- Style bias is model-specific: only 2/4 judges show significant formality bias\n- Likert sensitivity depends on axis observability: detects verbosity (surface-observable) but not formality even at n=350 (d=0.12)\n- GEE decomposition reveals confound gradient: verbosity=pure style (OR=13.10), formality=mixed, register=originality-dominated (OR=5.72)\n\n## Experiment design reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experiment plan includes 8 experiments:\n1. Multi-judge formality pairwise (4 judges on LLMBar, n=100)\n2. Cross-benchmark formality (AlpacaEval, MT-Bench, 3 judges) — all null after Holm correction, NLI pass drops to 72-78%\n3. Likert vs Pairwise sensitivity (systematic comparison) — GPT-4o verbosity Likert significant (p=0.008), breaking \"blindspot\" universality\n4. [not numbered in paper] — RATE control showing originality preference 55-64% across judges\n5. Scaled replication n=350 — Likert reaches significance p=0.030, d=0.12\n6. Register axis GPT-4o — BT NS, GEE shows originality-dominated\n7. Rewriter robustness — Llama NLI drops 16pp, BT goes NS\n8. GPT-4o verbosity scoring — Likert significant p=0.005, breaking blindspot for this cell\n\n## Your task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Soundness of methodology** (1-5): Is the CSD pipeline well-designed? Are the statistical methods appropriate? Are there methodological flaws?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor** (1-5): Are experiments well-controlled? Are conclusions supported by evidence? Are there confounds not addressed?\n\n3. **Specific issues to flag**:\n   - The NLI verification gate: is bidirectional entailment sufficient for content preservation? What about subtle semantic shifts NLI might miss?\n   - GEE model specification: only two predictors (style, originality). Are there omitted variables? Is the model underspecified?\n   - Sample sizes: n=88-100 for most cells. Power concerns?\n   - Cross-benchmark failure: is it pipeline failure or genuine null? The paper acknowledges this but doesn't resolve it.\n   - Rewriter dependence: 16pp NLI drop with Llama. How does this affect pipeline validity?\n   - Originality confound: 55-64% across judges. Is GEE sufficient to control for this?\n   - Scaled replication: Likert reaches p=0.030 at n=350 with d=0.12 — does this undermine the \"efficiency gap\" framing?\n   - Evidence matrix: only 3/5 evaluable cells show PW-only pattern, 1 shows Both, 2 inconclusive. Is this enough to support claims?\n   - BT estimate regression (63% → 59% at n=350): winner's curse implications?\n\n4. **Missing experiments**: What experiments would strengthen the paper? Consider whether the paper covers the planned experiments adequately.\n\n5. **Reproducibility** (1-5): Can results be reproduced? Is enough detail provided?\n\n6. **Preliminary scores**:\n   - Soundness: X/5\n   - Excitement: X/5  \n   - Reproducibility: X/5\n   - Overall Assessment: X/5 (use the ARR scale above)\n\nFormat your review as a structured report with sections: Summary, Strengths, Weaknesses, Questions for Authors, Missing Experiments, Scores. Be thorough and calibrated to EMNLP 2026 standards.","agentId":"a9d202cd973b194ce","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my thorough reading of the paper, project files, experimental decisions, and evidence matrix, here is my structured review.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Methodology Soundness and Experimental Rigor\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper proposes Counterfactual Style Decomposition (CSD), a pipeline for detecting per-axis style bias in LLM judges through style-controlled rewriting, NLI content gating, and position-controlled pairwise comparison. The central finding is the \"Likert Blindspot\": standard Likert scoring systematically fails to detect style biases that pairwise comparison reveals clearly. Evidence spans 2 judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B) x 3 axes (formality, verbosity, register), with GEE logistic decomposition revealing a confound gradient across axes. The paper makes a measurement-methodology contribution rather than a novel algorithmic one.\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1. Well-controlled experimental design with systematic robustness checks.**\nThe seven-method sensitivity hierarchy (Table 2) is the paper's strongest methodological contribution. By showing that ALL Likert-based methods fail (ATE, binarized, ordinal regression, GEE, Wilcoxon) while both pairwise methods succeed, the authors convincingly rule out test-choice confounds. The ordinal regression result (p=0.769) is particularly important---it eliminates the interval-scale assumption objection definitively.\n\n**S2. Cross-judge and cross-axis replication with consistent patterns.**\nThe 2x3 matrix with both proprietary (GPT-4o) and open-source (Qwen3-32B) judges, showing consistent Blindspot patterns, is methodologically sound. The verbosity axis (BT 76-77%, both judges) provides a near-ideal demonstration because the GEE originality component is non-significant (OR=0.44-0.49), meaning the entire observed effect is attributable to style.\n\n**S3. Appropriate statistical framework.**\nThe use of Bradley-Terry as primary analysis with pair-level bootstrap is correct for position-controlled pairwise data. The Holm-Bonferroni correction with explicit primary/exploratory/confirmatory designation shows statistical maturity. The paper properly acknowledges the Hoenig & Heisey (2001) critique and reframes the power curve as illustrative rather than post-hoc power analysis.\n\n**S4. Honest treatment of limitations.**\nThe paper forthrightly discusses evaluator-producer bias (Qwen3-32B as both rewriter and judge), the register axis weakness, and the quality-style confound identifiability limit. The GEE gradient (verbosity=pure style, formality=mixed, register=originality-dominated) is presented as a diagnostic finding rather than overclaimed.\n\n**S5. Direction-aware NLI verification is a genuine methodological insight.**\nRecognizing that bidirectional entailment is structurally incompatible with information-density-changing axes (recovering verbosity from 65% to 86% pass rate) demonstrates careful thinking about the measurement pipeline's validity conditions.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1. (Major) GEE model specification is underspecified with only two predictors.**\nThe GEE logistic model includes only `style` and `originality` as predictors. This is a severely underspecified model for the phenomena being studied. Omitted variables include: text length (even after style rewriting), text complexity/readability, topic/domain effects, and judge-specific position bias residuals (position is controlled by design but may interact with style). The massive ORs for verbosity (10.97-13.10) suggest possible confounding with length---verbose rewrites are necessarily longer, and length bias is the most documented LLM judge bias. The paper does not separate the verbosity style effect from the pure length effect, which is a critical omission given that LC-AlpacaEval specifically targets length as the dominant confound. Without a length covariate in the GEE model, the claim that verbosity represents \"pure style bias\" is not supported---it may equally be pure length bias relabeled.\n\n**W2. (Major) Sample sizes raise precision concerns despite adequate power for primary effects.**\nn=88-100 per cell is sufficient for the observed large effects (BT 63-77%), but the GEE decomposition requires adequate power to detect the *absence* of the originality confound. Claiming verbosity is \"confound-free\" because originality OR=0.49 with p=0.096 is a failure-to-reject-null claim. With n=86 (verbosity NLI-pass), the power to detect a meaningful originality effect (say OR=2.0) is modest. The CI for Qwen3-32B verbosity originality OR ([0.21, 1.13]) includes values up to 1.13---it cannot rule out a small originality confound. The paper should present this as \"no evidence of confounding\" rather than \"confound-free.\"\n\n**W3. (Major) NLI gate as content preservation verification has fundamental limitations the paper underacknowledges.**\nBidirectional NLI entailment tests whether propositions in A are entailed by B and vice versa. This is necessary but not sufficient for content preservation: (a) NLI operates at the propositional level and may miss pragmatic/implicature shifts; (b) quality perception can shift without propositional content changing (e.g., a poorly-structured formal sentence may be perceived as lower quality than a well-structured casual one); (c) the paper acknowledges this as \"quality perception shifts mediated by style itself\" in limitations, but this is not merely a limitation---it is a fundamental identifiability problem that affects the core claim. If the judge perceives formal text as higher quality *because of* formality (style mediates quality perception), then OR=2.21 for formality conflates style bias with legitimate quality assessment. The NLI gate cannot distinguish these.\n\n**W4. (Moderate) Cross-benchmark failure is inadequately addressed.**\nThe paper mentions only LLMBar as the evaluation benchmark, despite the experimental plan including AlpacaEval and MT-Bench. The project files reveal that NLI pass rates drop to 72-78% on these benchmarks, and results become non-significant after Holm correction. This is buried in the paper as a limitation (\"additional axes, larger benchmarks... needed\") but should be transparently reported as a negative result. Does CSD's Blindspot finding generalize beyond LLMBar? The paper cannot answer this, which limits the practical significance of the contribution.\n\n**W5. (Moderate) Rewriter dependence undermines generalizability claims.**\nThe project files mention that a Llama rewriter shows a 16pp NLI pass rate drop (from 88% to an implied ~72%). If the pipeline's validity depends critically on using Qwen3-32B as the rewriter, this constrains the practical applicability. The paper does not report any rewriter robustness experiment, despite it being in the experimental plan. This is an important omission---if different rewriters produce different counterfactual quality, the detected \"bias\" magnitude may be partially an artifact of rewriter-judge interaction.\n\n**W6. (Moderate) The \"Blindspot\" framing overstates what is essentially a well-known statistical power difference.**\nThe core finding---that forced-choice pairwise comparison has higher discriminability than rating scales---has been established since Thurstone (1927) and is well-documented in psychometrics. The paper acknowledges this in Related Work (\"Our contribution is not the discovery of this principle but the first systematic quantification\"), but the title, abstract, and framing continue to present this as a \"Blindspot\" discovery. At n=350 (from the experimental plan), Likert reaches significance (p=0.030, d=0.12). This confirms the finding is a power/sensitivity issue, not a structural blindspot. The paper's own power curve shows that at n~462, binarized Likert would detect the effect. Calling this a \"Blindspot\" rather than a \"sensitivity gap\" is a framing choice that borders on overclaiming.\n\n**W7. (Minor) BT estimate regression (63% from n=88 vs. planned ~60% at larger n) suggests winner's curse.**\nThe project's decision log (D032) shows the BT estimate evolved from 69.1% to 63.0% as methodology was corrected. The dream review imagines further regression to ~64.8% at n=400. This trajectory is consistent with winner's curse / regression to the mean at small samples. While 63% remains significant, the CI width (14.5pp) is large enough that the true effect could be as low as 55.8%---barely above chance in practical terms. The paper does not discuss this.\n\n**W8. (Minor) Evaluator-producer bias is inadequately controlled.**\nQwen3-32B serves as: (1) style classifier, (2) rewriter, (3) NLI validator (via LLM-as-annotator), and (4) one of two judges. This quadruple role creates layered confounds. The paper argues GPT-4o provides independent validation, but GPT-4o is only one of two judges, and the rewriting artifacts are common to both judges' inputs. A rewriter from a different model family would have been essential for the primary results.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\nQ1. Can you add a length covariate to the GEE model for the verbosity axis? If the verbosity style OR drops substantially after controlling for token count, the \"pure style bias\" claim needs revision.\n\nQ2. What happens to the Blindspot if you use a continuous Likert scale (e.g., 0-100 slider) instead of integer 1-10? Your mechanism posits that integer discretization produces ties---have you considered that API judges could be prompted for finer-grained scores?\n\nQ3. The direction asymmetry for formality (78.8% vs 54.5%) and register (89.3% vs 68.8%) suggests that what you're measuring may be partially \"judges prefer original text\" rather than \"judges prefer formal/academic text.\" Your GEE controls for this, but with only 2 predictors and moderate n, how confident are you that the decomposition is correctly specified?\n\nQ4. Why not report AlpacaEval/MT-Bench results, even as negatives? The cross-benchmark generalizability is critical for the practical implications you draw.\n\nQ5. The paper claims the Blindspot is \"inherent to Likert measurement\" (abstract). But at n=462 your own power curve predicts detection. Is this not an \"inherent\" property of small-sample Likert rather than Likert measurement per se?\n\n### Missing Experiments\n\n1. **Length-controlled GEE for verbosity**: Add token count as a third predictor in the verbosity GEE. This is essential to distinguish \"verbosity style preference\" from \"length bias\"---the most documented confound in LLM judges.\n\n2. **Non-Qwen rewriter with full pipeline**: The experimental plan includes this (exp-009) but it does not appear in the paper. Even a pilot with GPT-4o as rewriter on 50 pairs would address the evaluator-producer concern.\n\n3. **Cross-benchmark results**: Report AlpacaEval and MT-Bench results transparently, even if null after correction. The absence of these results leaves a major gap in generalizability.\n\n4. **Continuous/fine-grained Likert**: Prompt judges for scores on a 0-100 scale or request logprob-based continuous scores. If the Blindspot disappears with finer granularity, the mechanism claim is validated; if it persists, the finding is stronger.\n\n5. **Human validation of NLI gate on a random sample**: The current validation uses Qwen3-32B (same model family as rewriter). Even 30 human-annotated pairs would substantially strengthen the NLI gate validity claim.\n\n6. **Additional judges (Claude, Gemini)**: The plan includes 4 judges but only 2 are tested. This limits the \"generalizes across architecturally distinct judges\" claim to a single comparison.\n\n### Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | 3.5/5 | Core methodology is reasonable but GEE underspecification (W1), NLI identifiability limits (W3), and the verbosity-length confound are significant gaps. Statistical methods are generally appropriate. |\n| **Excitement** | 2.5/5 | The contribution is a systematic quantification of a known psychometric phenomenon (pairwise > rating scales) applied to LLM judges. Useful but limited novelty. The GEE gradient is more interesting but underdeveloped and only has 2 judges x 3 axes. |\n| **Reproducibility** | 3.5/5 | Good detail on statistical methods, NLI thresholds, bootstrap parameters. Pipeline stages are clearly described. However: Qwen3-32B rewriting prompts are not fully specified, API model versions are given only for GPT-4o, and the code/data are not released. |\n| **Overall Assessment** | 3.0/5 | Findings-level contribution. The Likert Blindspot finding is real and practically useful, but (a) limited novelty given psychometric literature, (b) scope restricted to 1 benchmark x 2 judges, (c) the verbosity-length confound unaddressed, and (d) several planned experiments missing from the paper. With the missing experiments and a more honest framing (sensitivity gap rather than \"Blindspot\"), this would be a solid Findings paper. For Main, would need either broader scope (4+ judges, multiple benchmarks) or a deeper methodological contribution (the GEE gradient could serve this role if developed with proper model specification). |\n\n**Overall Assessment: 3.0 (Findings-level)**\n\nThe paper identifies a real and practically relevant phenomenon (Likert insensitivity to style bias in LLM judges) and provides solid within-scope evidence. However, the novelty ceiling (domain transfer of established psychometric principles), limited scope (1 benchmark, 2 judges), and the unaddressed verbosity-length confound prevent a Main conference recommendation. The GEE decomposition gradient is the most novel aspect but needs stronger model specification and broader validation to serve as a standalone contribution."}],"totalDurationMs":175698,"totalTokens":78286,"totalToolUseCount":23,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":725,"cache_read_input_tokens":74217,"output_tokens":3341,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":725},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3341,"cache_read_input_tokens":74217,"cache_creation_input_tokens":725,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":725,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":10,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
